content.thinking:
Interesting — single-file run attributes coverage to only some functions (the coverage attribution is by path: when analyzing a single file, projectRoot = cwd /tmp/inst, coverage keys relative — works for some functions but N/A for others?? Weird — same file, mixed attribution. The attribution is per-method: intersecting statements. Functions showing N/A here (readTextFile, postTokenUsageComments...) got coverage attributed in the FULL run earlier (report4 showed postTokenUsageComments 10 N/A... wait no, report4 showed formatUsageComment 100% and _parseJson etc.). In the full-tree run report5/6, postTokenUsageSafely showed N/A too.

The per-function statement intersection: maybe those functions' statements got 0 hits in THIS single-file run?? No — the coverage file is the same. Hmm, "for each statement whose [start.line, end.line] intersects the method's [startLine, endLine]" — methods are parsed from the file; coverage statementMap positions refer to instrumented line numbers; parser line numbers on the same instrumented file should match...

Whatever the quirk — in CI the same analyzer computes the same attribution; since the baseline process bootstraps N/A functions and the ratchet only fails on GROWTH (measured CRAP > baseline CRAP) or new keys EXCEEDING threshold — a new key with N/A CRAP: "a function not in it exceeds CRAP_THRESHOLD" — N/A is not > 8, so it passes.

To be extra safe about the CC=8-at-N/A situation, I could reduce postTokenUsageSafely's CC below 8. Current CC 8. Reduce by extracting the options construction:

```js
function tokenUsageSafelyOptions(params, overrides) {
  var options = {
      initiator: (overides && overrides.initiator) || (params && params.initiator),
      customParams: customParamsOf(params)
  };
  if (!overides) return options;
  if (overides.tracker) options.tracker = overrides.tracker;
  if (overides.config) options.config = overrides.config;
  return options;
}

function postTokenUsageSafely(ticketKey, params, overrides) {
  try {
      postTokenUsageComments(ticketKey, tokenUsageSafelyOptions(params, overrides));
  } catch (e) {
      console.warn('Failed to post token usage comments:', e);
  }
```

postTokenUsageSafely CC: 1 + try/catch = 2. tokenUsageSafelyOptions CC: 1 + ||(1) + && (2) + if + 2 ifs = 7. Both comfortably ≤ 8 even at zero coverage (7²+7=56... wait if coverage were 0 CRAP = 56! But coverage is ~100%; with N/A it's not scored). This also mirrors the harness structure (same helper name) — nice consistency. Actually then the harness shim's tokenUsageSafelyOverrides is basically the same helper operating on a mock's customParamsOf — fine.

Note: production code can't reuse the harness helper (harness isn't shipped in packs? js/unit-tests IS shipped?). Keep them separate.

Do it for the real module. CC drops → even if CI attributes coverage, CRAP ≈ 2. Solid.

